# Line endings — LF everywhere, on every machine.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: this repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true,
# which rewrites LF to CRLF in the WORKING COPY on checkout. The index stayed
# clean LF, so nothing was ever pushed wrong — but several source pins read raw
# file bytes and match an exact "\n" (e.g. lib/public-diner-review-pins.test.ts
# asserting an import block's shape). Against a CRLF working copy those pins
# fail on correct code, and a real failure can hide behind the noise.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` makes git normalise to LF in the index AND check out LF,
# regardless of each machine's core.autocrlf setting. That is what the pins,
# the CRLF sweeps, and the Edit/Write tooling on this box all already assume.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files git must never touch. Without these, `text=auto` can corrupt a
# binary that happens to look text-ish to git's heuristic.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.node binary

# Windows-only scripts genuinely need CRLF — cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only .cmd
# and .bat files (a trailing LF can swallow the last command).
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
